### How does the exception table work?

Each method has an exception table in its bytecode. The JVM compiler (javac) generates it from your try-catch structure.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.sql.SQLException;

public class ExceptionTableDemo {

    // This method compiles to bytecode with an exception table
    public static String process(String tradeId) {
        try {
            return settle(tradeId);        // bytecode: PC 0-10
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            return "BAD_INPUT";             // handler at PC 13
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            return "RUNTIME_ERROR";         // handler at PC 20
        }
    }

    private static String settle(String id) {
        if (id.equals("BAD")) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid trade");
        return "SETTLED: " + id;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(process("TRD-001"));
        System.out.println(process("BAD"));

        // Exception table (from javap -c -v):
        // from  to  target  type
        //   0   10    13    IllegalArgumentException
        //   0   10    20    RuntimeException
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Exception table maps PC ranges to handlers");
        System.out.println("Try body (PC 0-10) is plain bytecode, ZERO overhead");
    }
}
```

```text
SETTLED: TRD-001
BAD_INPUT

Exception table maps PC ranges to handlers
Try body (PC 0-10) is plain bytecode, ZERO overhead
```

### Why does try have zero runtime cost?

The try body compiles to plain bytecode with no extra instructions. No markers, no branches, no registration. The exception table is metadata, never executed on the happy path.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class ZeroCostTryDemo {

    // With try-catch: SAME bytecode as without try-catch on happy path
    public static int withTryCatch(int a, int b) {
        try {
            return a + b;  // just: iload, iload, iadd, ireturn
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return -1;
        }
    }

    // Without try-catch
    public static int withoutTryCatch(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;  // same: iload, iload, iadd, ireturn
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int iterations = 1_000_000;

        long start = System.nanoTime();
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) withTryCatch(i, i);
        long withMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;

        start = System.nanoTime();
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) withoutTryCatch(i, i);
        long withoutMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;

        System.out.println("With try-catch: " + withMs + "ms");
        System.out.println("Without try-catch: " + withoutMs + "ms");
        System.out.println("Difference: negligible (try body is the same bytecode)");
    }
}
```

```text
With try-catch: 3ms
Without try-catch: 3ms
Difference: negligible (try body is the same bytecode)
```

### How do you inspect the exception table?

Use `javap -c -v` to disassemble a class file and see the exception table.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class JavapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("To see the exception table:");
        System.out.println("  javac ExceptionTableDemo.java");
        System.out.println("  javap -c -v ExceptionTableDemo.class");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Output includes:");
        System.out.println("  Exception table:");
        System.out.println("    from  to  target  type");
        System.out.println("      0   10    13    Class IllegalArgumentException");
        System.out.println("      0   10    20    Class RuntimeException");
    }
}
```

```text
To see the exception table:
  javac ExceptionTableDemo.java
  javap -c -v ExceptionTableDemo.class

Output includes:
  Exception table:
    from  to  target  type
      0   10    13    Class IllegalArgumentException
      0   10    20    Class RuntimeException
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **finally is an exception table entry too.** The finally block compiles to a handler for "any" type (catches all), ensuring cleanup runs.
- **JIT and try-catch.** The JIT compiler can still optimize code inside try blocks. The exception table does not prevent inlining or other optimizations on the happy path.
- **Nested try-catch.** Each nested try produces additional exception table entries. The JVM searches them in order: innermost first.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does try-catch slow down normal execution? (No, the try body is plain bytecode with zero overhead.)
- When is the exception table consulted? (Only when a throw actually happens.)
- How does the JVM find the right catch? (Matches PC range and exception type IS-A.)
- What happens if no handler is found? (Frame is popped, search continues in the caller.)
